    title: "ไม่ and ไหม: not, and yes/no questions",
    blurb: "Say what you don't like, ask yes/no questions, and answer them the Thai way.",
    deck: "Traps for English speakers",
    body: `
<p>English makes a negative with "do not" and a question by moving words around: "You like it" becomes "Do you like it?". Thai changes nothing in the sentence. It adds one small word: <b>ไม่</b> in front for "not", <b>ไหม</b> at the end for a yes/no question.</p>
<h3>ไม่: not, before the verb</h3>
<p>Put <b>ไม่</b> <i>mâi</i> right before the verb or the describing word. There is no "do": "not like" is simply <b>ไม่ชอบ</b>.</p>
{{ex:ผมไม่ชอบกาแฟ|phǒm mâi châawp gaa-faae|I don't like coffee. (male speaker)}}
{{ex:อาหารไม่เผ็ด|aa-hǎan mâi phèt|The food isn't spicy.}}
<p>To say something is not a noun ("not a teacher"), keep using <b>ไม่ใช่</b> from the lesson on เป็น.</p>
<h3>ไหม: a yes/no question at the end</h3>
<p>Add <b>ไหม</b> <i>mǎi</i> to the end of a statement and it becomes a yes/no question; a polite particle can follow it. Your voice does not rise as in English: every word keeps its own tone, and ไหม does the asking. In speech you will often hear ไหม said with a high tone.</p>
{{ex:เผ็ดไหม|phèt mǎi|Is it spicy?}}
{{ex:ชอบไหมคะ|châawp mǎi khá|Do you like it? (female speaker)}}
<h3>Answer with the verb</h3>
<p>Thai has no all-purpose "yes". To say yes, repeat the verb or describing word from the question. To say no, put ไม่ in front of it.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Question</th><th>Yes</th><th>No</th></tr><tr><td class="k">ชอบไหม</td><td class="k">ชอบ</td><td class="k">ไม่ชอบ</td></tr><tr><td class="k">เผ็ดไหม</td><td class="k">เผ็ด</td><td class="k">ไม่เผ็ด</td></tr><tr><td class="k">ไปไหม</td><td class="k">ไป</td><td class="k">ไม่ไป</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:ไม่ชอบครับ|mâi châawp khráp|No, I don't (like it). (male speaker)}}
<h3>ใช่: only for "is it…?"</h3>
<p><b>ใช่</b> <i>châi</i> means "that's right". Use it to confirm a fact, often after a question that ends in <b>ใช่ไหม</b> ("…, right?"). <b>ไม่ใช่</b> means "no, that's not it".</p>
{{ex:คุณเป็นครูใช่ไหม|khun bpen khruu châi mǎi|You're a teacher, right?}}
{{ex:ใช่ค่ะ|châi khâ|Yes, that's right. (female speaker)}}
<h3>หรือเปล่า: "or not?"</h3>
<p><b>หรือเปล่า</b> <i>rǔue-bplàao</i> at the end also asks a yes/no question, a bit like "…or not?". It is very common in speech, and you answer it the same way, with the verb.</p>
{{ex:ไปตลาดหรือเปล่า|bpai dtà-làat rǔue-bplàao|Are you going to the market?}}
<h3>Four words, four tones</h3>
<p>These four look and sound alike to English ears. Only the tone tells them apart.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Word</th><th>Tone</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td class="k">ไม่</td><td>falling</td><td>not</td></tr><tr><td class="k">ไหม</td><td>rising</td><td>yes/no question</td></tr><tr><td class="k">ใหม่</td><td>low</td><td>new</td></tr><tr><td class="k">ไม้</td><td>high, long vowel</td><td>wood</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:ไม้|máai|wood}}
{{ex:โต๊ะใหม่|dtó mài|a new table}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> answering every question with ใช่. Asked <b>ชอบไหม</b>, saying <b>ใช่</b> → <b>ชอบ</b> (or <b>ไม่ชอบ</b>). Answer with the verb; keep ใช่ for "is it…?".</div>
`,
