    id: "clusters",
    title: "Clusters and leading consonants",
    blurb: "Read two consonants that start one syllable, a silent ห or อ in front, and the vowel Thai does not write.",
    deck: "Directions and places",
    body: `
<p>Some Thai words start with two consonant letters. Sometimes both are said together, sometimes the first one is silent, and sometimes a vowel hides between them. This lesson shows how to tell which is which.</p>
<h3>True clusters: two letters, one syllable</h3>
<p>Like English <i>cl</i>, <i>pr</i> or <i>tr</i>, Thai can start a syllable with a consonant plus ร, ล or ว: <b>ครับ</b> <i>khráp</i>, <b>ปลา</b> <i>bplaa</i> (fish), <b>ขวา</b> <i>khwǎa</i> (right). The tone follows the class of the <b>first</b> letter, and a tone mark sits over the second one, as in <b>ใกล้</b> <i>glâi</i> (near).</p>
{{ex:เลี้ยวขวา|líiao khwǎa|Turn right.}}
{{ex:ตรงไป|dtrong bpai|Go straight.}}
{{ex:ตลาดอยู่ใกล้ๆ|dtà-làat yùu glâi-glâi|The market is close by.}}
{{ex:ไม่ไกล|mâi glai|It's not far.}}
<p>In casual speech many people drop the ร or ล of a cluster, so you will hear ครับ said without the r. The course writes and romanizes the full form.</p>
<h3>Leading ห: silent, but it changes the tone</h3>
<p>A ห written before a low-class letter that has no high-class partner (ง ญ น ม ย ร ล ว) is not said. Its job is to make that letter high class, so the tone rules for high-class letters apply. <b>หมา</b> <i>mǎa</i> (dog) has a rising tone; <b>มา</b> (come) is mid.</p>
{{ex:หมาตัวใหญ่|mǎa dtuua yài|a big dog}}
{{ex:ห้องน้ำอยู่ที่ไหน|hâwng-náam yùu thîi-nǎi|Where is the bathroom?}}
<p>The same silent ห is in <b>หนึ่ง</b> (one), <b>ไหน</b> (where, which) and <b>ใหญ่</b> (big).</p>
<h3>Leading อ: four words to know</h3>
<p>A silent อ before ย makes it mid class. Only four common words do this, so just learn them: <b>อยู่</b> <i>yùu</i> (to be at), <b>อยาก</b> <i>yàak</i> (to want), <b>อย่า</b> <i>yàa</i> (don't), <b>อย่าง</b> <i>yàang</i> (kind, way).</p>
{{ex:อยู่ที่ตลาด|yùu thîi dtà-làat|It's at the market.}}
<h3>Hidden vowels</h3>
<p>When two consonants cannot form a cluster, a short <i>a</i> is said between them but not written. The second syllable then takes the class of the first letter: <b>ตลาด</b> <i>dtà-làat</i> is read ตะ-หลาด, so ลาด gets the low tone of a mid-class dead syllable. The same happens in <b>สบาย</b> <i>sà-baai</i>, <b>ขนม</b> <i>khà-nǒm</i> and <b>ถนน</b> <i>thà-nǒn</i> (road), where นม and นน take the rising tone of high-class ข and ถ.</p>
{{ex:ถนนนี้|thà-nǒn níi|this road}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> saying the silent ห. <b>หมา</b> read with an h sound in front → <b>หมา</b> (mǎa), starting straight on the m. The ห only gives the rising tone.</div>
`,
