    title: "Special spellings and reading sentences",
    blurb: "Read silent letters, the common irregular words, ๆ and ฯ, and a menu line with no spaces.",
    deck: "Food and drink",
    body: `
<p>You can now read most Thai syllables with the rules. A handful of spellings break them, and they are common, so this lesson collects them. Then it puts everything together: reading a line with no spaces between words.</p>
<h3>Silent letters under ์</h3>
<p>The small mark ์ over a letter means "don't say this letter". It is frequent in words borrowed from English and from old Indian languages: <b>เบียร์</b> <i>biia</i> (beer) ends on the vowel, and <b>ไมค์</b> <i>mái</i> (the name Mike) has no k. Words borrowed from English also often take a high tone that the spelling does not show, as ไมค์ does; the romanization tells you.</p>
{{ex:เบียร์สองขวด|biia sǎawng khùuat|two bottles of beer}}
{{ex:ผมชื่อไมค์|phǒm chûue mái|My name is Mike. (male speaker)}}
<h3>ทร said s, and a silent ร</h3>
<p>In a few words ทร is said <i>s</i>: <b>ทราบ</b> <i>sâap</i>, a formal "to know". In <b>จริง</b> <i>jing</i> (true, real) the ร is simply not said.</p>
{{ex:ผมไม่ทราบครับ|phǒm mâi sâap khráp|I don't know. (formal, male speaker)}}
{{ex:จริงไหม|jing mǎi|Really? Is it true?}}
<h3>Vowel length the spelling hides</h3>
<p>Some very common words are spelled with a short vowel but said with a long one: <b>น้ำ</b> <i>náam</i> (water), <b>ได้</b> <i>dâai</i> (can, to get), <b>เก้า</b> <i>gâao</i> (nine). Others look long but are said short: <b>เล่น</b> <i>lên</i> (to play), <b>ต้อง</b> <i>dtâwng</i> (must), <b>เงิน</b> <i>ngoen</i> (money). There is no rule to spot them; the romanization always shows the length, so trust it.</p>
{{ex:น้ำสองแก้ว|náam sǎawng gâaeo|two glasses of water}}
{{ex:เงินสด|ngoen-sòt|cash}}
<h3>The repeat mark ๆ and the short mark ฯ</h3>
<p>ๆ repeats the word before it. It often softens or stresses the word, or shows more than one: <b>เด็กๆ</b> is "the kids", <b>ช้าๆ</b> is "slowly". The mark ฯ shortens a long name; you will meet it in <b>กรุงเทพฯ</b> <i>grung-thêep</i>, Bangkok, read without it.</p>
{{ex:เด็กๆเล่นที่บ้าน|dèk-dèk lên thîi bâan|The kids are playing at home.}}
{{ex:ช่วยพูดช้าๆ|chûuai phûut cháa-cháa|Please speak slowly.}}
<h3>Reading a line with no spaces</h3>
<p>Thai writes the words of a phrase together and puts a space only between phrases, where English would put a comma or a full stop. Don't sound out letter by letter. Look for words you know first, and the gaps between them fall into place. On a menu, each line is the dish, then the price.</p>
{{ex:ข้าวผัดกุ้ง 60 บาท|khâao-phàt gûng hòk-sìp bàat|shrimp fried rice, 60 baht}}
{{ex:ชาเย็น 40 บาท|chaa-yen sìi-sìp bàat|Thai iced tea, 40 baht}}
<p>In ข้าวผัดกุ้ง you can spot ข้าว (rice), then ผัด (fried), then กุ้ง (shrimp): the main word comes first. Prices are written with ordinary digits and read in Thai.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> sounding out every letter. <b>เบียร์</b> read with an r at the end → <b>เบียร์</b> (biia), because ์ silences the ร.</div>
`,
