    title: "Ordering food and drink",
    blurb: "Order dishes and drinks, say how spicy you want them, call the server and pay.",
    deck: "Food and drink",
    body: `
<p>Thai food is one of the best reasons to learn Thai, and ordering it takes only a few patterns you already know: the classifier, ไม่ and the polite particles.</p>
<h3>Asking for a dish: ขอ and เอา</h3>
<p>English says "Can I have…" or "I'll have…". Thai says <b>ขอ</b> <i>khǎaw</i> (may I have) + the dish, or the more casual <b>เอา</b> <i>ao</i> (I'll take) + the dish. Add the number and classifier after it, and ครับ or ค่ะ at the end. Dishes on a plate are counted with <b>จาน</b> <i>jaan</i>.</p>
{{ex:ขอผัดไทยสองจานครับ|khǎaw phàt-thai sǎawng jaan khráp|Two pad thai, please. (male speaker)}}
{{ex:เอาส้มตำไม่เผ็ดค่ะ|ao sôm-dtam mâi phèt khâ|I'll have papaya salad, not spicy. (female speaker)}}
<p>Dish names follow the same order as the rest of Thai: the main word first, then what describes it. <b>ข้าวผัดกุ้ง</b> is "rice, fried, shrimp", shrimp fried rice. So when you read a menu, the first word tells you what kind of dish it is (rice, noodles, curry), and the words after it tell you how it is cooked and what is in it. You do not need "a" or "the", and there is no plural: the number and classifier do that job.</p>
<h3>Drinks with their classifiers</h3>
<p>Drinks are counted by glass (<b>แก้ว</b>) or bottle (<b>ขวด</b>), after the number.</p>
{{ex:ขอกาแฟเย็นสองแก้วครับ|khǎaw gaa-faae yen sǎawng gâaeo khráp|Two iced coffees, please. (male speaker)}}
{{ex:ขอชาเย็นสองแก้วครับ|khǎaw chaa-yen sǎawng gâaeo khráp|Two Thai iced teas, please. (male speaker)}}
<h3>Tastes</h3>
<p>The four tastes you will be asked about: <b>เผ็ด</b> <i>phèt</i> (spicy), <b>เปรี้ยว</b> <i>bprîiao</i> (sour), <b>หวาน</b> <i>wǎan</i> (sweet), <b>เค็ม</b> <i>khem</i> (salty). To control the heat, say <b>ไม่เผ็ด</b> (not spicy) or <b>เผ็ดนิดหน่อย</b> (a little spicy). Adding <b>นะ</b> before the particle makes it a friendly request. And if it tastes good, say <b>อร่อยมาก</b>.</p>
{{ex:ไม่เผ็ดนะครับ|mâi phèt ná khráp|Not spicy, OK? (male speaker)}}
{{ex:เผ็ดนิดหน่อย|phèt nít-nàwi|a little spicy}}
{{ex:อร่อยมาก|à-ràwi mâak|It's delicious.}}
<h3>Calling the server</h3>
<p>In Thai restaurants people often call a younger server <b>น้อง</b> <i>náawng</i>, "younger sibling", with the particle: <b>น้องครับ</b> or <b>น้องคะ</b> (คะ, the high tone of questions, is also used when calling someone). <b>ขอโทษครับ</b> or <b>ขอโทษค่ะ</b> ("excuse me") works with anyone.</p>
{{ex:น้องคะ|náawng khá|Excuse me! (to a younger server, female speaker)}}
<h3>Paying and taking it away</h3>
<p>To ask for the bill, say <b>เช็คบิลด้วย</b>; ด้วย makes it softer. For takeaway, <b>ห่อกลับบ้าน</b> means "wrap it to take home".</p>
{{ex:เช็คบิลด้วยค่ะ|chék-bin dûuai khâ|Check, please. (female speaker)}}
{{ex:ห่อกลับบ้านครับ|hàaw glàp bâan khráp|To take away, please. (male speaker)}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> using the word for "hot" for spicy food. <b>ร้อน</b> means hot to the touch, like a hot drink. Ordering <b>ไม่ร้อน</b> for "not spicy" → <b>ไม่เผ็ด</b>.</div>
`,
